Z||000004||Oreif||03-24-2001||01:57 PM||||Cool looking engine. Can I get more info on what you did? Where did you find a manifold? What size carb? What other work has been done to the motor? Is it a 2.8L?||||reg||4
Z||000005||Taijiguy||03-24-2001||04:51 PM||||The whole car is tricked out big time, but the motor part breaks down like this:<BR>Just rebuilt last summer, 2.8 punched out .040 with new forged pistons, high volume high pressure oil pump, Heads completely reworked, ported, polished and gasket matched (shark fins smoothed and left intact) The exhaust manifolds have had the restrictions removed, and rewelded to increase their strength. The cat has been yanked and replaced with a straight pipe. Hoping for a true dual setup this summer. The cam is the wildest off the shelf grind I could locate, .443/288 intake .465/298 exh (those numbers will go to .472 and .496 with better springs and 1.6 roller rockers, also this summer) There's alot of lope in the cam, it will barely idle below 1000 rpm, but absolutely comes ALIVE at around 2400-2800 rpm. The intake is an Edelbrock, two pieces, which I had to order one piece from Summit, the other from Jegs. The carb is a Holley 4160, 390 CFM. I pulled and rebuilt a distributor from a 78 Blazer so I would have mechanical advance. An Accel Super Coil, & 8mm wires pretty much top it off. The only other notible changes back in the engine bay are the obvious reduction in wiring and hoses, and the 5 speed Getrag out of what was left of the cradle from some mystery-mobile. I modified the 4 speed shifter, and manufactured my own linkage on the trans. I managed to retain the four speed cables. It shifts about as accurate as the cable systems will allow. I did every little bit myself. Good or bad, everything on the car is my own work. (To my credit or blame) LOL I love the way it sounds with the carb on there. The tone is deeper and much more gutteral. I can't wait to hear it with the duals. I'll try to get some pics of the motor in the car up soon. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||03-24-2001||07:18 PM||||I don't usually get excited about the 60 degree V6's, but... ***drool***<P>About the sub, I'd go with a single 8" sub and bridge that amp to it. Nope, you will not need a cap with that setup.||||reg||1
